<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/16th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Spanish conquest of the Muisca</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1537">1537</a>–<a href="/years/1540">1540</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Spanish conquest of the Muisca was the subjugation of the Muisca Confederation of the Colombian highlands by conquistadors led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gonzalo-jimenez-de-quesada">Gonzalo Jiménez de Quesada</a> from 1537. The Spaniards defeated the rulers of Bacatá and Hunza and founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bogota">Santa Fe de Bogotá</a> in 1538. The region became the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-kingdom-of-egypt">New Kingdom</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a>, the core of later Spanish rule in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colombia">Colombia</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/spanish-conquest-of-the-muisca.jpg" alt="Spanish conquest of the Muisca" width="320" height="467" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Coriolano Leudo Obando · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Don_Gonzalo_Jim%C3%A9nez_de_Quesada.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The Muisca Confederation covered about 25,000 square kilometres of the Altiplano Cundiboyacense, with population estimates from 300,000 to 2,000,000. Its chief rulers were the zipa of Bacatá, the zaque of Hunza, the iraca of Sugamuxi and the Tundama, beside independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cacique">caciques</a>. Known as the Salt People for their salt works at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zipaquira">Zipaquirá</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nemocon">Nemocón</a> and Tausa, they also mined emeralds and worked traded gold. Tales of the initiation of a new zipa at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-guatavita">Lake Guatavita</a> reached the Spanish at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santa-marta">Santa Marta</a> and inspired an expedition in search of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-dorado">El Dorado</a>.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gonzalo-jimenez-de-quesada">Gonzalo Jiménez de Quesada</a> left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santa-marta">Santa Marta</a> in April 1536 with his brother Hernán Pérez de Quesada as second in command, part of the force by land and part by boat up the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magdalena-river">Magdalena River</a>. Of the men who set out, only 173 survived to reach Muisca territory 11 months later.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Quesada founded Chipatá on 8 March 1537 and entered a far better organised society than any the Spanish had met on the way. The zipa <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tisquesusa">Tisquesusa</a>, warned by the mohan Popón that he would die &quot;bathing in his own blood&quot;, left Bacatá for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nemocon">Nemocón</a>. After taking Hunza, Quesada went on to Suamox, the sacred City of the Sun; his men looted its Temple of the Sun of more than 300 kilograms of gold before two soldiers' torches set the building on fire.</p><p>Early in 1538 Quesada divided 40,000 pieces of fine gold and 562 emeralds among his men. On 6 August 1538, the date usually given, he founded Santafé de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bogota">Bogotá</a> with 12 houses of reed, and on 20 August, allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sagipa">Sagipa</a>, the last zipa, he defeated the Panche at the Battle of Tocarema. Other conquistadors, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nikolaus-federmann">Nikolaus Federmann</a> from the east and Sebastián de Belalcázar from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quito">Quito</a>, arrived meanwhile. In 1539 the Tundama gathered 10,000 guecha warriors to resist.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Quesada, Federmann and Belalcázar met at Bosa and sailed for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> in 1539, leaving Hernán as interim governor. The Spanish set up <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/encomienda">encomiendas</a>, gathered the Muisca into resguardos and pressed their conversion; the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of Santafé, Juan de los Barrios, ordered their temples destroyed, and the last public Muisca ceremony was held in Ubaque on 27 December 1563. Modern scholars have revised the conquerors' picture of the Muisca as warlike and of their confederation as a stratified empire.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Spanish conquest of the Muisca” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
